"Allah begins by swearing by the sun and its bright morning light. 'Duha' means the time when the sun has risen high and its light has spread out. With this oath, attention is drawn to the sun itself and to its most prominent feature, its light. For the sun is the source of light for the world, and by virtue of its light we see things and comprehend Allah's power.\n\nNext, an oath is sworn by the moon as it follows the sun. 'Tuluw' means to follow. The moon rises after the sun sets and reflects its light. Commentators have pointed to different aspects of this following: such as the moon following the sun in rising, following it in setting, receiving light from it, and being like a second sun when it is a full moon. Its clearest meaning is the time during full moon nights when the moon resembles the sun the most. This oath also draws attention to a different manifestation of light.\n\nAn oath is sworn by the day and the night. The day is the time when it reveals the sun clearly, while the night is the time when it conceals the sun. These two contrasting states emphasize the importance of light and the transience of darkness. While the brightness of the day demonstrates the presence of the sun, the darkness of the night makes one feel the value of light. There is also an allusion here to the luminous and dark states of the soul (nafs).\n\nAn oath is sworn by the sky and the One Who built it, and by the earth and the One Who spread it out. That is, Allah has raised the sky and placed the stars; and He has made the earth habitable and spread it out. Here, there are two views regarding whether the particle 'ma' is a relative pronoun (mawswl - 'who/the one who') or an infinitive/verbal noun (masdar - 'the building/spreading'). Its being a relative pronoun is more evident: 'He' is Allah. Although the earth is a sphere, the expression 'spread out' describes its being extended in such a way that one can live comfortably upon it.\n\nAn oath is sworn by the soul and the One Who proportioned it. That is, an oath by Allah, Who created the human soul, breathed the spirit into it, and fashioned its organs. Allah has inspired every soul with its wickedness and its righteousness (taqwa). Inspiration (ilham) means to cast something into the heart. Fujur means sin and rebellion, while taqwa means piety, caution, and goodness. Thanks to this inspiration, human beings can distinguish between good and evil.\n\nThe response to the oaths is this: Whoever purifies his soul has succeeded, and whoever corrupts and buries it has suffered loss. 'Tazkiyah' means to purify and develop; 'tadsiyah' means to corrupt and bury. This can occur through a person's own striving, just as it occurs through Allah's decree. While the Mu'tazilites emphasize human effort, Ahl al-Sunnah accepts both aspects. When the Prophet recited this verse, he would pray for the purification of his soul.\n\nThe story of the people of Thamud is an example of this. Thamud denied their prophet Salih because of their transgression. Salih (peace be upon him) told them to leave Allah's she-camel alone, but the most wretched among them killed the camel. Thereupon, Allah destroyed them with a mighty blast, an earthquake, or a thunderbolt, leveling them all equally. Allah is not questioned about what He does; He has no fear. This story demonstrates the end of those who corrupt their souls."